1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,812
For thirty years after Solvay, quantum mechanics was a tool.

2
00:00:03,812 --> 00:00:04,687
It worked.

3
00:00:04,687 --> 00:00:10,225
It predicted the spectra of atoms, the behaviour of semiconductors, the colour of the sky.

4
00:00:10,225 --> 00:00:17,200
Physicists used it every day and argued about its foundations at conferences, and the two activities barely touched.

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:24,450
Then in nineteen sixty-one, an IBM engineer named Rolf Landauer noticed something that should have been obvious, and wasn't.

6
00:00:24,450 --> 00:00:26,050
Information is physical.

7
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:30,525
If you store a bit, a one or a zero, you need something to store it in.

8
00:00:30,525 --> 00:00:33,175
A magnet, a charge, a photon.

9
00:00:33,175 --> 00:00:36,925
And if you erase that bit, you have to dump the information somewhere.

10
00:00:36,925 --> 00:00:43,762
Landauer calculated the cost: at least the thermal energy of the bit, multiplied by the natural log of two.

11
00:00:43,762 --> 00:00:44,937
A tiny number.

12
00:00:44,937 --> 00:00:46,050
But not zero.

13
00:00:46,050 --> 00:00:51,187
Erasing information is not free, because information is not abstract.

14
00:00:51,187 --> 00:00:53,812
It is a physical state of a physical system.

15
00:00:53,812 --> 00:00:55,800
To destroy it, you have to pay.

16
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:58,600
That idea travelled quietly for a decade.

17
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:10,262
In nineteen seventy-three, Charles Bennett at IBM took it further: if erasing is what costs energy, then a computation that never erases anything could, in principle, be free.

18
00:01:10,262 --> 00:01:11,937
Reversible computation.

19
00:01:11,937 --> 00:01:15,600
Logic gates that run backwards as easily as forwards.

20
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:17,850
Bennett was thinking about thermodynamics.

21
00:01:17,850 --> 00:01:19,112
He got something else.

22
00:01:19,112 --> 00:01:23,187
Because a reversible gate is a gate that keeps all of its inputs.

23
00:01:23,187 --> 00:01:25,025
It doesn't throw anything away.

24
00:01:25,025 --> 00:01:34,612
And in nineteen eighty, a young physicist named Paul Benioff pointed out that a quantum system, evolving according to Schrodinger's equation, is also reversible.

25
00:01:34,612 --> 00:01:37,225
The equation runs backwards perfectly.

26
00:01:37,225 --> 00:01:39,937
So why not build a computer out of quantum systems?

27
00:01:39,937 --> 00:01:42,162
Benioff's paper was almost ignored.

28
00:01:42,162 --> 00:01:44,787
It was too early, and it was too strange.

29
00:01:44,787 --> 00:01:46,987
But it was the first sketch of the idea.

30
00:01:46,987 --> 00:01:52,650
The idea got its voice in nineteen eighty-one, at a conference in Boston, from Richard Feynman.

31
00:01:52,650 --> 00:01:55,075
Feynman was not trying to invent a computer.

32
00:01:55,075 --> 00:01:57,000
He was trying to simulate physics.

33
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,562
And he had noticed a problem.

34
00:01:58,562 --> 00:02:06,687
A classical computer simulating a quantum system has to track every possible state, and the number of possible states grows exponentially.

35
00:02:06,687 --> 00:02:09,612
Thirty particles, and you are already out of memory.

36
00:02:09,612 --> 00:02:14,487
Three hundred, and you need more bits than there are atoms in the observable universe.

37
00:02:14,487 --> 00:02:16,975
Feynman's suggestion was almost cheeky.

38
00:02:16,975 --> 00:02:23,637
If a classical computer is too slow to simulate quantum mechanics, build a computer that is quantum mechanical.

39
00:02:23,637 --> 00:02:25,637
Let nature do the calculation.

40
00:02:25,637 --> 00:02:33,550
"Nature isn't classical, dammit," he said, "and if you want to make a simulation of nature, you'd better make it quantum mechanical."

41
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:34,700
The room laughed.

42
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:35,837
He wasn't joking.

43
00:02:35,837 --> 00:02:42,837
In nineteen eighty-five, David Deutsch, a young physicist at Oxford, turned Feynman's suggestion into a theory.

44
00:02:42,837 --> 00:02:52,487
He described a machine that could simulate any physical process, a universal quantum computer, and showed that it could do things no classical computer could do efficiently.

45
00:02:52,487 --> 00:02:54,237
Not just simulate physics.

46
00:02:54,237 --> 00:02:55,075
Compute.

47
00:02:55,075 --> 00:02:58,062
Deutsch's paper is where the field really begins.

48
00:02:58,062 --> 00:03:01,775
It is also where the trouble begins, because Deutsch was careful.

49
00:03:01,775 --> 00:03:03,450
He said the machine would work.

50
00:03:03,450 --> 00:03:05,175
He did not say how to build one.

51
00:03:05,175 --> 00:03:12,212
Then in nineteen ninety-four, a mathematician at Bell Labs named Peter Shor found the first thing that would actually be worth building.

52
00:03:12,212 --> 00:03:16,487
Shor was working on a problem that had been open for centuries: factoring.

53
00:03:16,487 --> 00:03:20,825
Given a large number, find the primes that multiply together to make it.

54
00:03:20,825 --> 00:03:23,762
Classical computers can do it, but slowly.

55
00:03:23,762 --> 00:03:27,450
Double the size of the number, and the time roughly doubles with it.

56
00:03:27,450 --> 00:03:33,075
For a six hundred digit number, the best algorithms would take longer than the age of the universe.

57
00:03:33,075 --> 00:03:37,450
Shor found a way to do it in polynomial time, if you had a quantum computer.

58
00:03:37,450 --> 00:03:41,287
Not a small improvement, an entirely different scaling law.

59
00:03:41,287 --> 00:03:47,737
A six hundred digit number becomes a few hours of work, on a machine with twenty million physical qubits.

60
00:03:47,858 --> 00:03:51,433
The reason it works is beautiful, and worth stating plainly.

61
00:03:51,433 --> 00:03:58,008
Factoring is really a question about periodicity: find the repeating pattern hidden in the powers of a number.

62
00:03:58,008 --> 00:04:05,895
And a quantum computer is very good at finding repeating patterns, because it can be in many states at once, and those states interfere.

63
00:04:05,895 --> 00:04:07,970
Interference is the whole trick.

64
00:04:07,970 --> 00:04:09,758
Not parallel universes.

65
00:04:09,758 --> 00:04:10,795
Not magic.

66
00:04:10,795 --> 00:04:13,008
Waves adding up and cancelling out.

67
00:04:13,008 --> 00:04:15,820
A quantum computer is an interference machine.

68
00:04:15,820 --> 00:04:21,645
It sets up a superposition in which every wrong answer cancels, and the right answer survives.

69
00:04:21,645 --> 00:04:22,645
Then you measure.

70
00:04:22,645 --> 00:04:27,970
Two years later, Lov Grover found a second trick: searching an unsorted list.

71
00:04:27,970 --> 00:04:30,783
A classical computer has to check every item.

72
00:04:30,783 --> 00:04:35,183
A quantum computer can do it in about the square root of the number of steps.

73
00:04:35,183 --> 00:04:39,345
For a billion items, that is thirty thousand steps instead of a billion.

74
00:04:39,345 --> 00:04:40,620
Not exponential.

75
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:41,795
Still enormous.

76
00:04:41,795 --> 00:04:47,108
By nineteen ninety-six, the field had a theory, two algorithms, and no hardware.

77
00:04:47,108 --> 00:04:54,283
It had mathematicians, computer scientists, and a growing number of physicists who suspected the whole thing was impossible.

78
00:04:54,283 --> 00:04:58,795
Because a quantum computer has a problem that no classical computer has.

79
00:04:58,795 --> 00:05:02,295
It is made of the same fragile stuff it is trying to compute with.

80
00:05:02,295 --> 00:05:05,920
And the moment it touches the outside world, it falls apart.

